    "patron": "Civil defence volunteers, adolescents, stress relief, and Pietrelcina (popular patronages; no Church decree found)",
    "relic_description": "<p>Padre Pio died in his cell at the Capuchin friary of San Giovanni Rotondo at about 2:30 a.m. on 23 September 1968, and about 100,000 people came to his funeral on 26 September. He was buried at San Giovanni Rotondo. His stigmata had faded before his death, and the doctor who examined his body found no wounds and no scars.</p>\n<p>The tomb was opened after midnight on 3 March 2008. Archbishop D’Ambrosio reported the body in fair condition: the skull partly skeletal, the chin and body well preserved, the hands looking as if manicured, and no visible stigmata. A silicone mask modeled on a 1968 photograph was made for the display. The body was shown from 24 April 2008, when Cardinal Saraiva Martins celebrated Mass for about 15,000 people, and the display was extended to September 2009. In April 2010 the remains were moved to a new crypt decorated in gold in the sanctuary at San Giovanni Rotondo.</p>\n<p>In February 2016, at the request of Pope Francis, his relics and those of St. Leopold Mandic were carried in procession down the Via della Conciliazione into St. Peter’s Basilica for the Jubilee of Mercy, and were there on Ash Wednesday, 10 February. Aleteia gives 5 to 11 February; another source gives 8 to 14 February. No miracle was claimed for the condition of the body; some headlines called it incorrupt, and the archbishop’s report did not.</p>",
    "relic_location": "Crypt of the sanctuary, San Giovanni Rotondo, Italy"
  },
  "related": {
    "miracles": [
      {
        "miracles": "<p>This list gathers the healings, visions, and other wonders attributed to Padre Pio, with their sources and their status. Two of them, the healings examined for his beatification and his canonization, were approved by the Church after medical and theological review. The others are the reports of witnesses, of his friends, of devotional writers, and of Padre Pio himself, and each entry says who reported it. The list makes no claim of scientific proof, and it does not dismiss any account. Where the sources disagree, or where a story is contested, the entry says so.</p>\n<h2 id=\"miracles-approved-by-the-church\">Miracles approved by the Church</h2>\n<h3 id=\"m01\">A ruptured thoracic duct healed, Salerno, 1995</h3>\n<p>Between 1 and 6 November 1995, Consiglia (Lina) De Martino of Salerno was healed of a ruptured thoracic duct without treatment. According to the account compiled for her case, a swelling under her left collarbone appeared on 1 November, two scans that day showed the rupture and a large collection of lymph, and surgery was set for 3 November. A perfume associated with Padre Pio was noticed on 1 and 2 November, and Fra Modestino of San Giovanni Rotondo prayed for her at Padre Pio’s tomb. On 3 November the swelling was gone; she later said that on the morning of 4 November she heard a voice tell her she was healed, and she left the hospital on 6 November without surgery. The medical board found the cure scientifically inexplicable by a unanimous vote on 30 April 1998, the theologians agreed on 22 June, and the cardinals and bishops on 20 October.</p>\n<p>Status: approved by the Church as the miracle for his beatification (decree of 21 December 1998).</p>\n<p>Source: Dicastery for the Causes of Saints, “Pio da Pietrelcina,” <a href=\"https://www.causesanti.va/it/santi-e-beati/pio-da-pietrelcina.html\">https://www.causesanti.va/it/santi-e-beati/pio-da-pietrelcina.html</a>; “The Miracles of Lina De Martino and Matteo Pio Colella,” caccioppoli.com, <a href=\"https://caccioppoli.com/28%20the%20miracles%20of%20Lina%20De%20Martino%20and%20Matteo%20Pio%20Colella.html\">https://caccioppoli.com/28%20the%20miracles%20of%20Lina%20De%20Martino%20and%20Matteo%20Pio%20Colella.html</a>; EWTN, “Highlights of the Cause for Padre Pio,” <a href=\"https://www.ewtn.com/catholicism/library/highlights-of-the-cause-for-padre-pio-13861\">https://www.ewtn.com/catholicism/library/highlights-of-the-cause-for-padre-pio-13861</a></p>\n<h3 id=\"m02\">A seven-year-old with meningitis, San Giovanni Rotondo, 2000</h3>\n<p>On 20 January 2000 Matteo Pio Colella, aged seven, fell ill in San Giovanni Rotondo with fulminant bacterial meningitis, and he recovered. The account compiled for his case says that he was the son of Dr. Antonio Colella, a doctor at Casa Sollievo della Sofferenza, and Maria Lucia Ippolito; that he went into septic shock and a coma; and that his heart stopped once. His mother prayed at Padre Pio’s tomb while the friars said the rosary, and she reported seeing a bearded friar lift up a child and noticing the scent of flowers. When the boy woke, he said that an old man with a white beard and a brown habit had told him he would get well quickly, and he picked out Padre Pio from a photograph.</p>\n<p>Status: approved by the Church as the miracle for his canonization (decree of 20 December 2001, per the Dicastery for the Causes of Saints; one English chronology gives other dates).</p>\n<p>Source: Dicastery for the Causes of Saints, “Pio da Pietrelcina,” <a href=\"https://www.causesanti.va/it/santi-e-beati/pio-da-pietrelcina.html\">https://www.causesanti.va/it/santi-e-beati/pio-da-pietrelcina.html</a>; “Biography,” Voce di Padre Pio, <a href=\"https://vocedipadrepio.com/en/biography/\">https://vocedipadrepio.com/en/biography/</a>; “The Miracles of Lina De Martino and Matteo Pio Colella,” caccioppoli.com, <a href=\"https://caccioppoli.com/28%20the%20miracles%20of%20Lina%20De%20Martino%20and%20Matteo%20Pio%20Colella.html\">https://caccioppoli.com/28%20the%20miracles%20of%20Lina%20De%20Martino%20and%20Matteo%20Pio%20Colella.html</a></p>\n<h2 id=\"during-padre-pios-life\">During Padre Pio’s life</h2>\n<p>These accounts come from Padre Pio’s own letters, from the friars and doctors who examined him, from the bishop sent by the Holy Office in 1921, and from friends and pilgrims. None of them was approved by a Church decree.</p>\n<h3 id=\"m03\">The stigmata, 1910 to 1968</h3>\n<p>Padre Pio later said that marks first appeared in his hands on 7 September 1910 at Piana Romana, near Pietrelcina; his first letter about them, in September 1911, describes a red mark the size of a coin in each palm, with sharp pain. On 20 September 1918, after Mass in the choir of Our Lady of Grace at San Giovanni Rotondo, wounds appeared in his hands, feet, and side, and they bled for fifty years. Dr. Romanelli (1919–1920) judged them not natural, Professor Bignami (1919) thought them produced by suggestion and kept open with iodine, Dr. Festa reported favorably to the Holy Office in 1925, and Father Gemelli (1920) called them hysterical without examining them. Bishop Rossi’s report of 1921 called them a real fact, neither fraud nor the devil’s work, while the Holy Office in 1923 found nothing supernatural established. The historian Luzzatto later revived a pharmacist’s testimony that Padre Pio had obtained carbolic acid; Padre Pio had told Rossi it was for sterilizing syringes. The wounds faded before his death, and the doctor found no scar.</p>\n<p>Status: studied by doctors and by the Holy Office; never the subject of a decree approving them as miraculous; contested. John Paul II spoke of the stigmata in his beatification homily.</p>\n<p>Source: “Pio da Pietrelcina,” Italian Wikipedia, <a href=\"https://it.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pio_da_Pietrelcina\">https://it.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pio_da_Pietrelcina</a>; “Biography,” Voce di Padre Pio, <a href=\"https://vocedipadrepio.com/en/biography/\">https://vocedipadrepio.com/en/biography/</a>; “San Giovanni Rotondo. Transverberation. Stigmata,” caccioppoli.com, <a href=\"https://caccioppoli.com/02%20San%20Giovanni%20Rotondo;%20Transverberation;%20Wounds%20Stigmata;%20examinations;.html\">https://caccioppoli.com/02%20San%20Giovanni%20Rotondo;%20Transverberation;%20Wounds%20Stigmata;%20examinations;.html</a>; “Padre Pio,” English Wikipedia, <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Padre_Pio\">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Padre_Pio</a>; John Paul II, homily for the beatification, 2 May 1999, <a href=\"https://www.vatican.va/content/john-paul-ii/en/homilies/1999/documents/hf_jp-ii_hom_02051999_padre-pio.html\">https://www.vatican.va/content/john-paul-ii/en/homilies/1999/documents/hf_jp-ii_hom_02051999_padre-pio.html</a></p>\n<h3 id=\"m04\">The transverberation, August 1918</h3>\n<p>From 5 to 7 August 1918, while he was hearing the confessions of the boys of the seraphic seminary at San Giovanni Rotondo, Padre Pio saw a heavenly figure holding a long iron blade with fire at its point, which pierced his soul. He sent the boy away. “I could barely let out a moan; I felt I was dying,” he wrote to his director, Father Benedetto, who understood the experience as a transverberation.</p>\n<p>Status: Padre Pio’s own account in a letter to his spiritual director.</p>\n<p>Source: “Pio da Pietrelcina,” Italian Wikiquote, <a href=\"https://it.wikiquote.org/wiki/Pio_da_Pietrelcina\">https://it.wikiquote.org/wiki/Pio_da_Pietrelcina</a> (Epistolario, 1995 ed., no. 1065); “Pio da Pietrelcina,” Italian Wikipedia, <a href=\"https://it.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pio_da_Pietrelcina\">https://it.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pio_da_Pietrelcina</a>; “Padre Pio,” English Wikipedia, <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Padre_Pio\">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Padre_Pio</a></p>\n<h3 id=\"m05\">A birth and a deathbed in Udine, 1905</h3>\n<p>According to a devotional account, in February 1905 the young friar wrote a note saying that he had seen himself in Udine, at the bedside of a dying father while a girl was being born. The girl, Giovanna Rizzani Boschi, was born on 18 January 1905, and she later gave testimony in the process for his beatification.</p>\n<p>Status: reported bilocation; recorded by a devotional site; not confirmed by the Church.</p>\n<p>Source: “Close Encounters of a Special Kind with Padre Pio around the World,” caccioppoli.com, <a href=\"https://caccioppoli.com/Close%20encounters%20of%20a%20special%20kind%20with%20Padre%20Pio%20around%20the%20world,%20without%20leaving%20the%20convent..html\">https://caccioppoli.com/Close%20encounters%20of%20a%20special%20kind%20with%20Padre%20Pio%20around%20the%20world,%20without%20leaving%20the%20convent..html</a></p>\n<h3 id=\"m06\">Ecstasy and levitation, 1905 to 1909</h3>\n<p>During the years of his illness as a young friar, one of his brothers claimed to have seen him in ecstasy and raised above the ground. His followers later spoke of levitation and the gift of tongues among his gifts.</p>\n<p>Status: reported by a single friar; popular tradition.</p>\n<p>Source: “Padre Pio,” English Wikipedia, <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Padre_Pio\">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Padre_Pio</a></p>\n<h3 id=\"m07\">Twenty days on Communion alone, Venafro, 1911</h3>\n<p>In 1911, when Padre Pio was confined to bed at the friary of Venafro, one friar said that he lived for more than twenty days on the Eucharist alone.</p>\n<p>Status: reported by a single witness.</p>\n<p>Source: “Padre Pio,” English Wikipedia, <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Padre_Pio\">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Padre_Pio</a>; “Pio da Pietrelcina,” Italian Wikipedia, <a href=\"https://it.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pio_da_Pietrelcina\">https://it.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pio_da_Pietrelcina</a></p>\n<h3 id=\"m08\">General Cadorna, November 1917</h3>\n<p>A popular story tells of an appearance by Padre Pio to General Cadorna in November 1917. The versions give different dates, 9 November and 12 November, and no contemporary source for the story was found.</p>\n<p>Status: popular tradition; likely legend.</p>\n<p>Source: “Close Encounters of a Special Kind with Padre Pio around the World,” caccioppoli.com, <a href=\"https://caccioppoli.com/Close%20encounters%20of%20a%20special%20kind%20with%20Padre%20Pio%20around%20the%20world,%20without%20leaving%20the%20convent..html\">https://caccioppoli.com/Close%20encounters%20of%20a%20special%20kind%20with%20Padre%20Pio%20around%20the%20world,%20without%20leaving%20the%20convent..html</a></p>\n<h3 id=\"m09\">The perfume</h3>\n<p>Many people reported a perfume associated with Padre Pio, sometimes far from San Giovanni Rotondo. Bishop Rossi, the Holy Office’s visitor in June 1921, noted a strong and pleasant fragrance like violets in his cell, looked for its source, and found only ordinary soap. His blood was said to smell of violets, roses, or lilies. Asked about the perfume, Padre Pio called it “just sweets for the children.”</p>\n<p>Status: reported by many witnesses, including the Holy Office’s visitor; not investigated as a miracle.</p>\n<p>Source: “San Giovanni Rotondo. Transverberation. Stigmata,” caccioppoli.com, <a href=\"https://caccioppoli.com/02%20San%20Giovanni%20Rotondo;%20Transverberation;%20Wounds%20Stigmata;%20examinations;.html\">https://caccioppoli.com/02%20San%20Giovanni%20Rotondo;%20Transverberation;%20Wounds%20Stigmata;%20examinations;.html</a>; “Padre Pio,” English Wikipedia, <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Padre_Pio\">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Padre_Pio</a>; John McCaffery, <em>The Friar of San Giovanni</em> (1978), ch. III, p. 9, <a href=\"https://ia601501.us.archive.org/11/items/friar-of-san-giovanni/Friar_of_San_Giovanni_text.pdf\">https://ia601501.us.archive.org/11/items/friar-of-san-giovanni/Friar_of_San_Giovanni_text.pdf</a></p>\n<h3 id=\"m10\">Knowledge of hearts, and what he told Bishop Rossi, 1921</h3>\n<p>Asked about bilocation by Bishop Rossi in 1921, Padre Pio said that he did not know how it happened: whether his mind, some representation of him, or his body was present elsewhere. He also told the bishop that he sometimes perceived other people’s sins or virtues clearly. In his report of 4 October 1921 Rossi described the many healings people claimed as unconfirmed.</p>\n<p>Status: Padre Pio’s own statements to the Holy Office’s visitor; the healings were not confirmed.</p>\n<p>Source: “Padre Pio,” English Wikipedia, <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Padre_Pio\">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Padre_Pio</a></p>\n<h3 id=\"m11\">A fever of 48 degrees, 1920</h3>\n<p>Dr. Giorgio Festa recorded a body temperature of 48 °C in Padre Pio in 1920.</p>\n<p>Status: popular and disputed.</p>\n<p>Source: “Pio da Pietrelcina,” Italian Wikipedia, <a href=\"https://it.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pio_da_Pietrelcina\">https://it.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pio_da_Pietrelcina</a></p>\n<h3 id=\"m12\">Luigi Orione in St. Peter’s, 1923 or 1925</h3>\n<p>Luigi Orione, a priest later canonized, reported seeing Padre Pio in St. Peter’s in Rome while Padre Pio was at his friary. One source places it on 29 April 1923, at the beatification of Thérèse of Lisieux, and another in 1925.</p>\n<p>Status: reported bilocation; not confirmed by the Church.</p>\n<p>Source: “Close Encounters of a Special Kind with Padre Pio around the World,” caccioppoli.com, <a href=\"https://caccioppoli.com/Close%20encounters%20of%20a%20special%20kind%20with%20Padre%20Pio%20around%20the%20world,%20without%20leaving%20the%20convent..html\">https://caccioppoli.com/Close%20encounters%20of%20a%20special%20kind%20with%20Padre%20Pio%20around%20the%20world,%20without%20leaving%20the%20convent..html</a>; “Pio da Pietrelcina,” Italian Wikipedia, <a href=\"https://it.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pio_da_Pietrelcina\">https://it.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pio_da_Pietrelcina</a></p>\n<h3 id=\"m13\">A farmer from near Padua, after the Second World War</h3>\n<p>McCaffery met a farmer from near Padua on a train who told him that, after a farm accident, he had developed clots in both lungs and was dying. He prayed for someone closer to God to intercede, saw a bearded monk at his bedside who put a hand on his chest and vanished, and was cured, as his doctors confirmed. Months later he recognized the monk in a photograph in a house in Padua, went to San Giovanni Rotondo the same night, and after his confession Padre Pio asked him how his lungs were.</p>\n<p>Status: reported by the man himself to John McCaffery; not investigated by the Church.</p>\n<p>Source: John McCaffery, <em>The Friar of San Giovanni</em> (1978), ch. I, pp. 1–3, <a href=\"https://ia601501.us.archive.org/11/items/friar-of-san-giovanni/Friar_of_San_Giovanni_text.pdf\">https://ia601501.us.archive.org/11/items/friar-of-san-giovanni/Friar_of_San_Giovanni_text.pdf</a></p>\n<h3 id=\"m14\">Father Dominic’s cancer</h3>\n<p>Father Dominic, an American Capuchin at San Giovanni Rotondo, told McCaffery that Padre Pio had cured him of cancer.</p>\n<p>Status: reported by the man himself; shrine tradition.</p>\n<p>Source: John McCaffery, <em>The Friar of San Giovanni</em> (1978), ch. II, pp. 5–6, <a href=\"https://ia601501.us.archive.org/11/items/friar-of-san-giovanni/Friar_of_San_Giovanni_text.pdf\">https://ia601501.us.archive.org/11/items/friar-of-san-giovanni/Friar_of_San_Giovanni_text.pdf</a></p>\n<h3 id=\"m15\">Father Dominic’s father, Milwaukee</h3>\n<p>Father Dominic’s father in Milwaukee asked his son to beg Padre Pio to help him at his death, and Padre Pio nodded when asked. Father Dominic’s sister later told him that their father, who could not walk without help, was found in his bed rather than on the veranda where they had left him, with a Host on his tongue.</p>\n<p>Status: reported bilocation, told to McCaffery by Father Dominic.</p>\n<p>Source: John McCaffery, <em>The Friar of San Giovanni</em> (1978), ch. XII, pp. 33–34, <a href=\"https://ia601501.us.archive.org/11/items/friar-of-san-giovanni/Friar_of_San_Giovanni_text.pdf\">https://ia601501.us.archive.org/11/items/friar-of-san-giovanni/Friar_of_San_Giovanni_text.pdf</a></p>\n<h3 id=\"m16\">A woman from Hawaii, and the perfume</h3>\n<p>McCaffery met an American woman from Hawaii on the train to Foggia who had come to see Padre Pio and was afraid to go on. At Foggia they met Father Dominic off the same train. The next day she told McCaffery she had smelled a strong perfume in her room that she had not brought, and Father Dominic said it was the time he had spoken to Padre Pio about her: “Quick off the mark.”</p>\n<p>Status: reported by a witness; not investigated.</p>\n<p>Source: John McCaffery, <em>The Friar of San Giovanni</em> (1978), ch. II, pp. 4–6, <a href=\"https://ia601501.us.archive.org/11/items/friar-of-san-giovanni/Friar_of_San_Giovanni_text.pdf\">https://ia601501.us.archive.org/11/items/friar-of-san-giovanni/Friar_of_San_Giovanni_text.pdf</a></p>\n<h3 id=\"m17\">A girl born without pupils, Sicily, 1947</h3>\n<p>Gemma di Giorgi, a Sicilian girl born without pupils, was brought to Padre Pio by her grandmother in 1947. She is reported to have seen afterward, including a steamboat on the sea, and Padre Pio is reported to have told the grandmother that the child could see.</p>\n<p>Status: popular account, from a 1999 book by Malachy Carroll cited in an encyclopedia; no medical file found.</p>\n<p>Source: “Padre Pio,” English Wikipedia, <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Padre_Pio\">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Padre_Pio</a></p>\n<h3 id=\"m18\">A prophecy to Karol Wojtyla, 1947</h3>\n<p>A story says that when the young Polish priest Karol Wojtyla visited Padre Pio in 1947, Padre Pio foretold that he would reach the highest post in the Church; one version says he called him “your holiness.” Stickler said it was true, Dziwisz denied it, Weigel’s biography leaves it out, and John Paul II himself denied it.</p>\n<p>Status: contested.</p>\n<p>Source: “Padre Pio,” English Wikipedia, <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Padre_Pio\">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Padre_Pio</a>; “Pio da Pietrelcina,” Italian Wikipedia, <a href=\"https://it.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pio_da_Pietrelcina\">https://it.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pio_da_Pietrelcina</a></p>\n<h3 id=\"m19\">A lamp that would not light, 1968</h3>\n<p>When a BBC crew under Mischa Scorer filmed Padre Pio’s Mass shortly before his death, they set one lamp to shine straight onto the altar. The next morning, Scorer told McCaffery, every lamp worked except that one. McCaffery took it as a sign that Padre Pio would not have his Mass disturbed.</p>\n<p>Status: told to McCaffery by the producer; McCaffery’s own interpretation.</p>\n<p>Source: John McCaffery, <em>The Friar of San Giovanni</em> (1978), ch. XLVIII, pp. 127–128, <a href=\"https://ia601501.us.archive.org/11/items/friar-of-san-giovanni/Friar_of_San_Giovanni_text.pdf\">https://ia601501.us.archive.org/11/items/friar-of-san-giovanni/Friar_of_San_Giovanni_text.pdf</a></p>\n<h2 id=\"after-padre-pios-death\">After Padre Pio’s death</h2>\n<h3 id=\"m20\">The wounds gone, 23 September 1968</h3>\n<p>McCaffery wrote that the stigmata had faded gradually before Padre Pio died. After his death the attending doctor found no wounds and no scars.</p>\n<p>Status: reported by witnesses; not presented by the Church as a miracle.</p>\n<p>Source: John McCaffery, <em>The Friar of San Giovanni</em> (1978), pp. 125–126, <a href=\"https://ia601501.us.archive.org/11/items/friar-of-san-giovanni/Friar_of_San_Giovanni_text.pdf\">https://ia601501.us.archive.org/11/items/friar-of-san-giovanni/Friar_of_San_Giovanni_text.pdf</a>; “Padre Pio,” English Wikipedia, <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Padre_Pio\">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Padre_Pio</a></p>\n<h3 id=\"m21\">The opening of the tomb, 2008</h3>\n<p>When the tomb was opened after midnight on 3 March 2008, Archbishop D’Ambrosio reported the body in fair condition: the skull partly skeletal, the chin and body well preserved, the hands looking manicured, and no visible stigmata. A silicone mask made from a 1968 photograph was used for the display.</p>\n<p>Status: official report of the exhumation; no miracle was claimed. Some headlines used the word “incorrupt,” and the archbishop’s report did not.</p>\n<p>Source: “Padre Pio,” English Wikipedia, <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Padre_Pio\">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Padre_Pio</a>; Catholic News Agency, “Padre Pio Exhumed Body Is Well Preserved,” 3 March 2008, <a href=\"https://www.catholicnewsagency.com/news/padre_pio_exhumed_body_is_well_preserved\">https://www.catholicnewsagency.com/news/padre_pio_exhumed_body_is_well_preserved</a></p>\n<p>Other accounts are named in our sources without enough detail to tell, among them a story of Padre Nazareno and a dying tertiary, and further healings collected by devotional writers.</p>",
        "time_period": [
          "modern_era"
        ]
      }
    ],
    "teachings": [
      {
        "teachings": "<p>Padre Pio wrote no books. His teaching survives in letters, written mostly because his spiritual directors told him to write, and in short sayings that people remembered and that the Church quotes. The letters fill four volumes of his <em>Epistolario</em>: to his directors (1910–1922), to Raffaelina Cerase (1914–1915), to his spiritual daughters (1915–1923), and to others. They are still under copyright, so the passages below are short, quoted from the Italian given in Church documents and the Italian Wikiquote, in our own English.</p>\n<h2 id=\"prayer\">Prayer: where God is found</h2>\n<p>For Padre Pio prayer came before learning. Books can describe God, he said, but only prayer reaches him. He called prayer a weapon and a key, the one means everyone has.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>“In books we look for God; in prayer we find him. Prayer is the key that opens God’s heart.”</p>\n<p><em>“Nei libri cerchiamo Dio, nella preghiera Lo troviamo. La preghiera è la chiave che apre il cuore di Dio.”</em></p>\n<p><cite>— Padre Pio, as quoted by the Dicastery for the Causes of Saints (our translation)</cite></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>John Paul II quoted a related saying at his canonization: “La preghiera è la migliore arma che abbiamo, una chiave che apre il Cuore di Dio” (prayer is the best weapon we have, a key that opens the heart of God). He lived it at the altar and in the confessional, and in 1942, answering Pius XII’s appeal for prayer for peace, he asked his spiritual children to form the groups that became his prayer groups, about seven hundred of them by his death.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-cross\">The Cross: where love is learned</h2>\n<p>The center of his spirituality was the Cross. He wrote that Jesus had told him that love is learned beneath it, and he described Calvary as the mountain the saints climb. To Raffaelina Cerase, on the feast of the Assumption in 1914, he wrote that a chosen soul is led by no other road than the one Christ walked.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>“Under the Cross one learns to love.”</p>\n<p><em>“Sotto la Croce s’impara ad amare.”</em></p>\n<p><cite>— Padre Pio, recording words he heard from Jesus, <em>Epistolario</em> I, p. 339 (our translation)</cite></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>He lived this literally: from 1918 to the last months of his life he bore wounds in his hands, feet, and side, which he covered and almost never spoke of.</p>\n<h2 id=\"suffering-as-a-gift\">Suffering offered as love</h2>\n<p>Padre Pio did not treat suffering as something merely to be endured. In his letters it is a gift he can give back to Christ: in love Christ delights him, and in sorrow he delights Christ. To his spiritual daughters he promised that a heart that holds the crucified Christ will find its crosses changed.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>“Keep Jesus crucified in your heart, and all the crosses of the world will seem to you like roses.”</p>\n<p><em>“Tieni nel tuo cuore Gesù crocifisso e tutte le croci del mondo ti sembreranno rose.”</em></p>\n<p><cite>— Padre Pio, letter to a spiritual daughter, <em>Epistolario</em> III, p. 827 (our translation)</cite></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>He told McCaffery that the nights were the worst, because if he slept the pain of the wounds grew; and he went on saying Mass and hearing confessions.</p>\n<h2 id=\"abandonment\">Abandonment and trust</h2>\n<p>His letters also record long stretches of darkness, when he wrote, “I look for God, but where am I to find him?” His counsel to others was surrender, like a small child carried by its mother.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>“Give yourselves up wholly to the divine heart of Jesus, like a child in its mother’s arms.”</p>\n<p><em>“Abbandonatevi pienamente sul cuore divino di Gesù, come un bimbo tra le braccia della madre.”</em></p>\n<p><cite>— Padre Pio, a saying quoted by John Paul II at the beatification, 2 May 1999 (our translation)</cite></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>To Raffaelina Cerase he wrote that it seemed Jesus had nothing else in hand but to sanctify her soul.</p>\n<h2 id=\"obedience\">Obedience</h2>\n<p>Padre Pio wrote his letters, kept silent, and accepted restrictions because his superiors asked it of him. He told one of them that he acted only to obey.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>“I act only to obey you.”</p>\n<p><em>“Opero solamente per ubbidirvi.”</em></p>\n<p><cite>— Padre Pio to a superior, <em>Epistolario</em> I, p. 807, as quoted by John Paul II, 2002 (our translation)</cite></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>In 1920 he would not show his wounds to Father Gemelli without written authorization, and from 1931 to 1933, when the Holy See allowed him only a private Mass, he said nothing in public in his own defense.</p>\n<h2 id=\"humility-and-vainglory\">Humility and the danger of vainglory</h2>\n<p>He knew the temptation that fame brings. In his letters he called the wish to be admired a martyrdom, and he described himself only as a poor friar who prays. When a Jesuit wondered how his humility survived the crowds, he laughed and said that a man who claimed God’s gifts as his own would have to be either mad or a thief, like a traveler who shows off as his own a watch he was asked to carry to Milan for repair.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>“My God! What a martyrdom the temptation of vainglory is.”</p>\n<p><em>“Dio mio! che martirio è la tentazione della vanagloria.”</em></p>\n<p><cite>— Padre Pio, letter, <em>Epistolario</em> (1995 ed.), 314 (our translation)</cite></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The watch story is told by John McCaffery in <em>The Friar of San Giovanni</em> (1978); the words above are our summary, not a quotation.</p>",
        "time_period": [
          "modern_era"
        ]
      }
    ],
