  "gaps": [
    "Biography length is about 6,900 words, below the tier A target of 8,000–10,000. The three note files do not support more without padding or invented scenes; a longer life needs the Epistolario, Luzzatto, Castelli, or Fr Agostino’s diary.",
    "No passage of the Epistolario was read in the printed text. All quotations come from the Dicastery page, the papal homilies, and Italian Wikiquote (1995 numbering, unclear whether page or letter number).",
    "The letter of 22 Oct 1918 (the stigmata of 20 Sept 1918) is summarized, not quoted; the Italian original was not read.",
    "The words to Don Salvatore Pannullo (“tutto nel nascondimento”) come from Italian Wikipedia without a named source. They are used in the hook and chapter 4 with “he is remembered to have said” and flagged in A Note on the Sources.",
    "Date conflicts stated in A Note on the Sources: first marks 1910 vs stigmata 1918; Bignami 26 vs 12 July 1919; Festa 9 vs 28 Oct 1919; 1931 decree 23 May vs 9 June; army discharge March 1918 vs March 1917; Orione sighting 1923 vs 1925; canonization-miracle dates (Dicastery 20 Dec 2001 vs EWTN 20 Dec 2000).",
    "Nihil obstat for the cause: Dicastery 29 Nov 1982 (used) vs caccioppoli.com 29 Nov 1981. Canonization decree: Dicastery 26 Feb 2002 vs EWTN 28 Feb 2002 (not stated in the text).",
    "Left out on the caller’s instruction or because unverified: Pius XI’s “badly informed” remark; “Resta con me, Signore”; caccioppoli.com items marked check (Spanish flu, nine days of silence after 20 Sept 1918, Pannullo’s 1918 remark, Archbishop Kenealy, Bastianelli, Merla, Festa’s question, “given for decoration”); Wikiquote humor sayings from third-hand sources.",
    "A few scene details were checked directly in McCaffery’s text at the pages the notes cite (pp. 1–3, 4–6, 33–34, 45–46, 62–65, 78–79, 125–128): the Padua farmer’s story, the Hawaii woman, Father Dominic’s father, the 1956 address to the doctors, the request not to disturb anyone, and the plea for the community’s pardon. They are paraphrased.",
    "The 1960 visitation, microphones, John XXIII’s “straw idol” note, and the funding claims rest on encyclopedia summaries of Luzzatto and others.",
    "Patronages are popular (Wikipedia), not official. “Patron Saints” category and patron field are marked traditional.",
    "Cadorna story: only a devotional source; dates conflict; Cadorna’s dismissal date not checked. Marked likely legend.",
    "Padre Nazareno and the dying tertiary: named in the notes without details; not written up.",
    "Images: no licence or rights page verified; no upload.",
    "Quotes: 16 saved, fewer than the 28 requested, because only these have a locator in a source the researchers read. Paul VI and John Paul II lines are not saved as quotes of the saint."
